1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:04,052
This is Exchange Korean Rules, version two point one.

2
00:00:04,272 --> 00:00:13,379
The tool now separates three jobs: Install prepares verified
installation files, Get inspects servers, and Set applies the workaround.

3
00:00:13,599 --> 00:00:18,449
Install does not install SQL Server or apply anything to Exchange.

4
00:00:18,669 --> 00:00:26,313
This is custom automation of published guidance, not a
knowledge base article, signed hotfix, or permanent product fix.

5
00:00:26,533 --> 00:00:31,577
The examples are illustrative. Nothing in this video changes an Exchange server.

6
00:00:32,772 --> 00:00:36,748
On GitHub, open the current guide on main and check the version.

7
00:00:36,968 --> 00:00:45,573
The active folders now keep the current scripts and latest source
kit. Older releases and compatibility commands are in the archive.

8
00:00:45,793 --> 00:00:52,798
The source zip goes to your browser's download location.
Use Show in folder, then extract the complete kit.

9
00:00:53,018 --> 00:00:57,314
Keep the shared module and private folder alongside the three scripts.

10
00:00:57,534 --> 00:01:06,226
Downloading does not stage files on Exchange. Update your existing
working folder deliberately; you do not need a new versioned folder.

11
00:01:07,438 --> 00:01:15,852
Running Install with no arguments now shows examples and
exits. It does not prompt, download, elevate, or write files.

12
00:01:16,072 --> 00:01:23,535
To prepare the files, use elevated sixty-four-bit Windows
PowerShell five point one and deliberately choose a source.

13
00:01:23,755 --> 00:01:30,026
Download explicitly fetches about seven hundred
forty-nine megabytes of Microsoft media.

14
00:01:30,246 --> 00:01:40,490
The output directory is optional and defaults to a unique location. A
management workstation is recommended; an Exchange host warns about resource use

15
00:01:40,490 --> 00:01:41,924
rather than blocking.

16
00:01:42,144 --> 00:01:47,752
Install only prepares files. It does not install SQL or apply the workaround.

17
00:01:48,938 --> 00:01:57,844
The first positional argument is an existing executable or source
folder. The optional second argument is a new output directory.

18
00:01:58,064 --> 00:02:06,840
A folder must directly contain the expected executable or the
rule files. The tool does not recursively search subfolders.

19
00:02:07,060 --> 00:02:13,702
If media and rules coexist, specify the exact
executable or use Rule Source Directory.

20
00:02:13,922 --> 00:02:21,023
Paths with spaces and matching pasted quotation marks
are accepted literally, without evaluating expressions.

21
00:02:21,243 --> 00:02:28,954
For a download destination, use Download with Output
Directory. A bare folder is never treated as download consent.

22
00:02:30,147 --> 00:02:37,131
A matching filename or displayed version is not
enough. Size, hash, and Microsoft signature must pass.

23
00:02:37,351 --> 00:02:46,501
An identity error now shows what was found and what is required. A
much smaller file is consistent with an incomplete or corrupt copy.

24
00:02:46,721 --> 00:02:53,662
Obtain a fresh complete copy rather than bypassing
verification or opening a routine Support case.

25
00:02:53,882 --> 00:02:58,881
Downloads keep a partial filename until identity and signature checks pass.

26
00:02:59,101 --> 00:03:07,119
Failures show one concise message and exit one. Automation
can request a catchable error with Error Action Stop.

27
00:03:08,313 --> 00:03:12,909
A successful build does not populate the source folder's payload directory.

28
00:03:13,129 --> 00:03:19,229
Use the returned Payload Directory with Set, or
change to the returned Expanded Package folder.

29
00:03:19,449 --> 00:03:27,680
If installation files are missing, the error now names the expected
folder and files and explains how to prepare or select them.

30
00:03:27,900 --> 00:03:33,539
Remote Apply validates the payload on the
calling computer before connecting to a target.

31
00:03:33,759 --> 00:03:39,335
Get does not need those installation files,
and an incomplete package fails explicitly.

32
00:03:40,522 --> 00:03:46,321
Get checks the exact Exchange build, Korean
WordBreaker identity, and rule-file state.

33
00:03:46,541 --> 00:03:51,562
It has no Apply mode and never adds Exchange rules or restarts services.

34
00:03:51,782 --> 00:03:56,623
A local interactive run can request normal Windows UAC elevation.

35
00:03:56,843 --> 00:04:03,466
Automation and remote sessions still need the required
rights; those protections have not been removed.

36
00:04:03,686 --> 00:04:13,060
The result shows one observed Status column. Eligibility is only a
prerequisite. It does not diagnose the problem or prove recovery.

37
00:04:14,272 --> 00:04:21,095
Expected existing-rule and incompatible-identity
results are now yellow skips, not fatal errors.

38
00:04:21,315 --> 00:04:26,304
Set leaves that server unchanged and continues inspecting the remaining list.

39
00:04:26,524 --> 00:04:37,022
An incompatible result explains the found and required build, DLL
version, size, or hash. The same explanation is in Applicability Reason.

40
00:04:37,242 --> 00:04:45,273
Existing files are never overwritten. A partial pair needs
investigation, and presence alone does not prove recovery.

41
00:04:45,493 --> 00:04:54,515
Routine skips give practical next steps, not blanket Support
instructions. Genuine operation or recovery failures still stop Set.

42
00:04:55,730 --> 00:05:06,231
Get and Set accept computer names as the first positional argument. For a
CSV, explicitly use CSV Path; the script does not guess from a positional

43
00:05:06,231 --> 00:05:06,874
filename.

44
00:05:07,094 --> 00:05:12,138
Native Name and FQDN properties now work without renaming a column.

45
00:05:12,358 --> 00:05:18,873
The importer chooses Computer Name first,
otherwise FQDN, then Name, once for the whole file.

46
00:05:19,093 --> 00:05:25,021
A blank or invalid selected value stops the
run instead of falling back to another column.

47
00:05:25,241 --> 00:05:36,011
The entire roster is validated before contact. Remote Get uses existing
Kerberos access and writes code staging and reports, not Exchange rule files.

48
00:05:37,230 --> 00:05:41,143
One to three computers retain the detailed per-server display.

49
00:05:41,363 --> 00:05:48,737
At four or more, the tool suppresses those blocks and progress
dumps, then shows totals by status and the report paths.

50
00:05:48,957 --> 00:05:53,192
This is a display change only. No target data is discarded.

51
00:05:53,412 --> 00:06:00,931
Errors still identify the failed server, and restarted
rollouts still require recovery input after every server.

52
00:06:01,151 --> 00:06:06,694
Explicit JSON and Pass Through output remain complete, regardless of list size.

53
00:06:07,897 --> 00:06:12,036
Dollar report contains typed PowerShell rows for every target.

54
00:06:12,256 --> 00:06:17,735
Format or filter it when you want details that compact output deliberately hides.

55
00:06:17,955 --> 00:06:24,161
Dollar report Files holds the output paths. Pass
Through supports explicit pipeline assignment.

56
00:06:24,381 --> 00:06:31,227
Normal runs retain the objects in your existing
session, including after the script's own UAC handoff.

57
00:06:31,447 --> 00:06:40,384
An unrelated external PowerShell process cannot populate its
parent's variables. Use its exports or explicit JSON instead.

58
00:06:41,605 --> 00:06:49,572
Normal runs export all three formats to a unique folder on
the caller, using the existing report root for compatibility.

59
00:06:49,792 --> 00:06:54,685
CSV is convenient for spreadsheets. JSON keeps structured detail.

60
00:06:54,905 --> 00:07:01,602
For Splunk file monitoring, use the finalized JSON
Lines file, not the rewritten rollout checkpoint.

61
00:07:01,822 --> 00:07:07,247
Do not combine search-time and indexed JSON extraction for the same source type.

62
00:07:07,467 --> 00:07:16,394
The customer's Splunk administrator must validate event count,
timestamps, and duplicates. No live customer integration is claimed.

63
00:07:17,605 --> 00:07:22,434
Set defaults to Apply. Start with What If on a still-eligible installation.

64
00:07:22,654 --> 00:07:30,543
Local preview validates applicability and payload without
changing files, restarting services, or exporting reports.

65
00:07:30,763 --> 00:07:36,157
At the default PowerShell preference, a
normal Set run no longer needs Confirm false.

66
00:07:36,377 --> 00:07:41,144
Add Confirm to request a prompt; stricter caller preferences are still honored.

67
00:07:41,364 --> 00:07:46,598
That does not remove UAC consent, maintenance approval, or the recovery gate.

68
00:07:46,818 --> 00:07:51,562
Remote What If only plans the roster. It does not inspect remote state.

69
00:07:52,772 --> 00:07:57,291
After the actual health checks and approved window, apply to one pilot.

70
00:07:57,511 --> 00:08:04,751
Restart Search must be requested explicitly. Without
it, Set only stages files and returns exit ten.

71
00:08:04,971 --> 00:08:13,140
The approved restart affects Host Controller and its content-processing
work, not an automatic restart of Transport or Store.

72
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:17,784
Do not promise zero delivery impact or a fixed outage duration.

73
00:08:18,004 --> 00:08:25,575
The tool checks the exact Content Engine process for a
bounded stability period. That is not workload validation.

74
00:08:26,772 --> 00:08:30,900
Use a mailbox whose active database is on the changed server.

75
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:38,894
Verify new ordinary and Korean messages deliver and become
searchable, with unique terms and a negative control.

76
00:08:39,114 --> 00:08:45,726
Check the original affected workload, fresh errors,
and queues. Assess historical backlog separately.

77
00:08:45,946 --> 00:08:51,490
If checks fail, stop and preserve evidence. Stale callers may need investigation.

78
00:08:51,710 --> 00:09:00,188
An event provider name alone does not identify the service. If
a caller needs recovery, follow a separately approved procedure.

79
00:09:01,397 --> 00:09:06,909
After the pilot passes, use Set with a
reviewed list and the explicit restart options.

80
00:09:07,129 --> 00:09:12,097
A restarted remote rollout is serial and requires an interactive console.

81
00:09:12,317 --> 00:09:18,223
Validate each server, then enter the exact
requested recovered statement before the next one.

82
00:09:18,443 --> 00:09:25,844
Compact output does not hide or skip that gate. Errors
stop the run and unvisited targets remain Not Run.

83
00:09:26,064 --> 00:09:33,880
The old Fleet wrapper remains compatible, but its Apply still
implies a restart. Use the new commands for new procedures.

84
00:09:35,063 --> 00:09:42,231
Zero can mean eligible detection, a preview, or a
completed operation. It is not proof of recovery.

85
00:09:42,451 --> 00:09:48,156
One is an error. Ten means Search restart is still required, not a Windows reboot.

86
00:09:48,376 --> 00:09:59,350
Twenty means an identity or existing-rule skip needs review. A skipped-only
run never claims restart pending. If a mixed file-only run actually stages

87
00:09:59,350 --> 00:10:00,959
rules, it returns ten.

88
00:10:01,179 --> 00:10:08,089
Preserve reports and the protected operation receipts.
Partial failures are not automatically rolled back.

89
00:10:08,309 --> 00:10:18,821
Support escalation remains appropriate for uncertain partial operations or
unstable restarted processes. Rollback still requires the owned receipt,

90
00:10:18,821 --> 00:10:23,111
unchanged identities, and actual Microsoft Support approval.

91
00:10:24,313 --> 00:10:33,163
The two point one source release passed three hundred twenty-one tests.
The later repository archive cleanup passed three hundred twenty-six.

92
00:10:33,383 --> 00:10:42,809
Native lab checks exercised the installer input cases and confirmed that
skipped targets no longer prevent later servers from being inspected.

93
00:10:43,029 --> 00:10:53,246
That is not a new workload-recovery pilot. Earlier EWS results did not
certify Outlook, web access, historical backlog, or production load.

94
00:10:53,466 --> 00:10:56,973
Start with the current guide on main and the complete package.

95
00:10:57,193 --> 00:11:04,963
Prepare verified files, inspect, preview, pilot carefully,
and expand only after the workload evidence passes.
